<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Pechenegs</h1><p class="st-meta">860 CE – 1091 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Turkic nomads who dominated the Black Sea steppe for two centuries, killed Sviatoslav of Kiev and were crushed by Byzantium in 1091.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Pechenegs lived between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-river">Ural</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> drove them west around 890. Allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, they expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> from their lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> around 895 and took over the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> describes eight tribes, each with its own chief, grouped into two wings either side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> paid them to hold back the Rus' and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, and they controlled the Dnieper rapids, where in 972 their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurya">Kurya</a> killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav I</a> and made his skull into a drinking cup.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Defeated outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> in 1036, and pressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, many Pechenegs moved into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. In 1091 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a> and his Cuman allies annihilated them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-levounion">Levounion</a>, and a final defeat in 1122 ended them as a people. Survivors settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pechenegs.jpg" alt="Pechenegs" width="320" height="168"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Pechenegs_defeating_the_Rus,_from_the_Skyllitzes_Matritensis,_fol._173r,_(Public_Domain)_via_Creative_Commons.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>860 CE – 1091 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kurya, Kegen, Tyrach</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pecheneg</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ukraine, Russia, Romania, Moldova</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Patzinaks, Pecheneg confederation</dd></dl></aside></div>
